{"authored" [{"question" "q-319886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & \\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "442e8807297dc2494084b95676f504ca51d5b48601a0223f31f0024d65b046fe" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-319886" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\sqrt{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 42 & 0 & -21 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "442e8807297dc2494084b95676f504ca51d5b48601a0223f31f0024d65b046fe" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-144"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 42 & 0 & -21 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 42 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 18 & 9 \\\\ 21 & 7 & -42 \\\\ 8 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 18 & 9 & -42 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-42001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 42 & 0 & -21 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $3\\times 3$ matrices, and  ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=A{I}_{3}B$\n- $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB$\n- ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $A{I}_{3}B=(A{I}_{3})B=AB.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{3})=AB+A{I}_{3}=AB+A.$ However,  $AB+A\\ne AB,$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  ${I}_{3}A=A{I}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "442e8807297dc2494084b95676f504ca51d5b48601a0223f31f0024d65b046fe" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/state.json" "file_sha256" "5847a665e3d8835b0f7b5fc99a5435f269aed8ba5aac82bc9c4cc21d5f2bcba6" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4411.json" "file_sha256" "4272b005767be26669bcaf030ca3970beb7632f2176cf544ab0ed70202140102" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-CB & =(A-C)B \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4411.json" "file_sha256" "4272b005767be26669bcaf030ca3970beb7632f2176cf544ab0ed70202140102" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4412.json" "file_sha256" "570a82dbe16295fb2f5d395185cba64f0011d454f11ea88f65dcfdae90d069cc" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4412.json" "file_sha256" "570a82dbe16295fb2f5d395185cba64f0011d454f11ea88f65dcfdae90d069cc" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A, B,$ and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n\n- $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n\n- $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4413.json" "file_sha256" "427e7d428a810cf7eb2e033e8e4e14b50a07cb6d6f607a52219d9438c336b57a" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-4413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\nAB + AC  \\neq BA + CA.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/example-4413.json" "file_sha256" "427e7d428a810cf7eb2e033e8e4e14b50a07cb6d6f607a52219d9438c336b57a" "basis" 1055 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993704/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e9943478b35891f7ec97098737cb707e521dc2c7b6bebffb4d2f4b02bd7dc95" "basis" 1224 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e9943478b35891f7ec97098737cb707e521dc2c7b6bebffb4d2f4b02bd7dc95" "basis" 1224 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/1403